<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="67432" href="schedule/2018/fall/EALC/550/67432.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2018" href="schedule/2018.xml">2018</calendarYear><term id="120188" href="schedule/2018/fall.xml">Fall 2018</term><subject id="EALC" href="schedule/2018/fall/EALC.xml">East Asian Language and Culture</subject><course id="550" href="schedule/2018/fall/EALC/550.xml">Seminar in EALC</course></parents><sectionNumber>GPP</sectionNumber><sectionTitle>JAPN Cult/Hist/Lit</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Recycling the classics: A Multimedia History of Adaptation in Japanese Cultural History  Adaptation is common in many cultures but in few other cases has the past been so consistently and compulsively re-used as in Japanese cultural history. This course explores how and why the classics have been adapted in writing, art, film, animation, and material practices from medieval times to the present day. We will look at a broad range of primary works in different media and learn to use concepts such as adaptation, parody, canonization, nation building, etc. in cultural analysis.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2018-08-27Z</startDate><endDate>2018-12-12Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>03:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>W      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>1140</roomNumber><buildingName>Foreign Languages Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Persiani" firstName="G">Persiani, G</instructor></instructors></meeting><meeting id="1"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>02:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>M      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>1040</roomNumber><buildingName>Foreign Languages Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Persiani" firstName="G">P